      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To compare the electrophysiological effects of two contrast media (CM), the non-ionic dimer iodixanol and the ionic dimer ioxaglate using computerised dynamic vectorcardiography (VCG) during coronary angiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study was designed as a double-blind, three-period crossover, randomised comparison between iodixanol (320 mg I/ml) and ioxaglate (320 mg I/ml). Group 1 (HVV) received ioxaglate (H) in the first injection in the left coronary artery (LCA) and iodixanol (V) in the following injections. Group 2 (VHH) received iodixanol in the first injection in LCA and ioxaglate in the following injections. The first three injections in the LCA were subjected to electrocardiographic analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "For five out of six VCG variables, there was a significant difference in response between iodixanol and ioxaglate. For these five variables, the deviations from baseline were greater in the ioxaglate than in the iodixanol group (P<0.05). The most pronounced effects from ioxaglate were seen on the ST-segment and T-wave."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Iodixanol caused less pronounced electrophysiological changes than ioxaglate, especially during the repolarisation phase. Vectorcardiography is a sensitive and reproducible technique for detecting electrophysiological effects induced by CM."
        }
      ]},
